feat(scraper): T-403 G1+G2+G4 — SEBI and company-host rungs, honest four-rung chain

G1 SEBI rung (sebi-source.ts). Listings verified live 2026-08-28 by probing smid
and reading each listing's own rows: smid=10 Draft Offer Documents (DRHP), 11
Red Herring (RHP), 12 Final Prospectus. Two traps in the markup, both handled:
the row href is a DETAIL PAGE (the PDF costs a second GET and lives under
/sebi_data/attachdocs/), and the row title embeds a NESTED anchor for the
Abridged Prospectus, so naive parsing yields a SUMMARY instead of the filing.
Company match is exact-key then fuzzy at >=0.85, refusing ambiguity.
parseSebiDetailPdfUrl accepts only attachdocs, never commondocs. Consulted only
when both exchanges failed, and never for a type SEBI does not host.

G2 company-host rung (company-host-source.ts). The website comes off the filing
cover, which we already extract for the M1 company check, so it costs nothing
extra. Intermediaries are rejected by CONTEXT, not a domain list: merchant banks
cannot be enumerated (a new one appears with every issue) but the cover always
labels whose website it is, and each match is bounded to its own label region so
"Registrar Website: ... Company Website: ..." still resolves to the issuer. At
most 3 GETs (R12). Only links whose anchor text or filename CLASSIFY are taken.

Chittorgarh is a VERIFIER ONLY, enforced structurally: extractVerifierLinks
drops anything not on an exchange/SEBI host, so no later code has to remember.

G4 the chain records every rung it tried, in order, with an outcome — including
the ones it skipped and why (not_served_by_sebi, no_company_url,
no_verifier_url, exchanges_settled_it, download_disabled). BLOCKED_ALL asserts
four rung entries; a short chain is a bug in the chain, not evidence the
document is unreachable, so it is logged loudly and the row stays retryable.
Escalation does NOT fire when the exchanges merely said "not filed yet" (F3).

Also: a structurally broken PDF (0 readable pages, the live SEBI DRHP case) is
REJECTED as unreadable_pdf, distinct from a scanned filing with no text layer,
which is still correctly skipped (E4).

TWO REAL BUGS the chain tests caught:
1. `outcome` was left unassigned when every download failed, so applyOutcome
   returned undefined and the state write threw three frames away. strict:false
   does not catch that. It is now initialised, and applyOutcome has a default
   arm that throws where the mistake actually is.
2. The G4 guard fired when downloads were disabled (no rung could run), masking
   a genuine BLOCKED_ALL. That case now records its own skip reason.

Tests: +16 SEBI, +14 company-host/verifier, +7 full-chain.
